# Lead for the right reasons
People can tell when you are fake or selfish. 

# Know who you are. Be in the right place before you lead. 
- You cannot lead when you do not believe in the leaders above you. 
- You don't need to agree with every decision, but you need to believe in their intent. 
- If you cannot quit your leadership role, you are not a leader, you are a manager. 

# Put the welfare of your team first. 
- If you can't put your team first, it's time to step away. 
  - This happens regularly. Retirement is a perfect example. 
- Add to your team wisely
  - An empty position is better than a wrongly filled position
  - Interview for strengths and character. Tolerate gaps.
  
# Be humble.
- Check your ego (Extreme Ownership, chapter 4)
- You never have to bring the solution to the table. 
- You have to find the solution with your team. 
- Built right, your team will carry you. 
- Never take credit when you can give it away. You will get credit for your team. 

# Only apologize if you are authentic (The Next Conversation).
- Do not apologize for doing your job, taking time, or taking space.
  - Instead, thank the other person for their accommodation. 
- Apologize when you've caused harm. Then, fix it. Be sincere. Some things cannot be fixed. You will have to live with them. 
- Your guilt is the price you pay for your growth. 

# Your job isn't to be liked- but it helps. 
- Be kind, not nice (The Next Conversation)
- Your team matters more than one individual (The Hard Thing about Hard Things)
  - If you don't feel bad when you fire someone, you are a sociopath
  - If you don't follow through and fire individuals when necessary, you hurt everybody else. 
  
# Take ownership
- This does not mean to do everything.
  - It means that when you are assigned a task, you ensure that it does get done, by you or someone else. 
- Everything your team must accomplish, you own. 
- Every failure means:
  - You assigned the wrong person
  - You did not train them well enough
  - You didn't clarify the assignment
  - You didn't provide the resources
  - You didn't provide the time

# You can not simply tell individuals to take ownership
- This is a ethos built over a lifetime. 
- To move a team in this direction
  - Admit your failures
  - Apologize as appropriate
  - Eat last (About Face)
  - Look out for the welfare of your team members both "on the job" and away from it. (Extreme Ownership, About Face)

